That was terrific.

27
00:03:47,094 --> 00:03:48,338
Thank you! That was...

28
00:03:48,362 --> 00:03:49,372
That was really great.

29
00:03:49,396 --> 00:03:51,374
Thank you. Do I know you?

30
00:03:51,398 --> 00:03:53,765
Not likely, my man.

31
00:03:53,833 --> 00:03:55,345
Well, you play
a hell of a guitar.

32
00:03:55,369 --> 00:03:56,868
Hey, thanks. You play?

33
00:03:56,937 --> 00:03:58,948
No, no, no, no, not like that.

34
00:03:58,972 --> 00:04:01,473
You're Eddie Cougar
Haynes, aren't you?

35
00:04:01,542 --> 00:04:04,910
You recorded a couple
albums in the-the late '50s.

36
00:04:04,978 --> 00:04:06,622
Played all the jazz
clubs in the '60s,

37
00:04:06,646 --> 00:04:08,358
and-and you disappeared
in the early '70s.

38
00:04:09,616 --> 00:04:12,784
Man, it's good
somebody remembers.

39
00:04:12,852 --> 00:04:15,486
Yeah, well, grab your
gear, I'll stake you to a meal.

40
00:04:16,589 --> 00:04:18,089
Yeah?

41
00:04:18,158 --> 00:04:19,424
Absolutely.

42
00:04:19,492 --> 00:04:21,126
All right. You got it.

43
00:04:21,194 --> 00:04:23,754
All right, I'll grab
your bag here.

44
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:28,912
Hey, hey, hey. Oh,
this is a pleasure.

45
00:04:28,936 --> 00:04:30,868
Let's go! All right.

46
00:04:40,948 --> 00:04:41,880
Whoo!

47
00:04:41,949 --> 00:04:43,882
Man! Is this yours?

48
00:04:43,951 --> 00:04:45,061
Oh, yeah.

49
00:04:45,085 --> 00:04:47,619
What'd you do, rob a bank?

50
00:04:47,687 --> 00:04:49,887
Something like that.

51
00:04:49,956 --> 00:04:51,757
Ever hear of Dreams?

52
00:04:51,825 --> 00:04:53,758
Dreams?

53
00:04:53,827 --> 00:04:54,926
No, I don't think so.

54
00:04:54,994 --> 00:04:56,427
Ah, it's my band.

55
00:04:56,496 --> 00:04:58,129
Yeah, it's how I got all this.

56
00:04:58,198 --> 00:04:59,876
We were together
about eight years.

57
00:04:59,900 --> 00:05:02,167
Made ten albums...
Seven platinum, two gold.

58
00:05:02,235 --> 00:05:03,501
Ooh!

59
00:05:03,570 --> 00:05:05,336
A lot of fun while it lasted.

60
00:05:05,405 --> 00:05:06,816
Boy... now I
produce a little and,

61
00:05:06,840 --> 00:05:08,651
you know, write a few songs.

62
00:05:09,710 --> 00:05:10,942
I bet you do.

63
00:05:11,011 --> 00:05:12,944
Come on. Let's go.

64
00:05:17,216 --> 00:05:18,416
Terry, you're late.

65
00:05:19,920 --> 00:05:21,898
I did the grocery
shopping for you.

66
00:05:21,922 --> 00:05:23,255
Eddie, this is my mother.

67
00:05:23,323 --> 00:05:25,022
Lorraine Maslin, Eddie Haynes.

68
00:05:25,091 --> 00:05:26,357
How do you do?

69
00:05:26,426 --> 00:05:27,837
Eddie's one of the
world's greatest blues

70
00:05:27,861 --> 00:05:29,761
guitarists. How nice.

71
00:05:29,829 --> 00:05:31,096
Don't forget...

72
00:05:31,164 --> 00:05:33,176
Your uncle's coming to
my house for dinner tonight.

73
00:05:33,200 --> 00:05:34,844
I'll bring him over
here about 9:00.

74
00:05:34,868 --> 00:05:37,336
Fine.

75
00:05:37,404 --> 00:05:38,537
Oh, Terry.

76
00:05:38,605 --> 00:05:40,483
The band's waiting
for you by the pool.

77
00:05:40,507 --> 00:05:42,319
Thanks for the shopping, Mom.

78
00:05:42,343 --> 00:05:45,444
Hey look, man,
maybe I better go.

79
00:05:45,512 --> 00:05:46,711
No, no, no. Forget it.

80
00:05:46,780 --> 00:05:48,713
Come on. Come on in.

81
00:05:55,789 --> 00:05:57,389
Here we go.

82
00:05:57,457 --> 00:05:58,923
My, my!

83
00:05:58,992 --> 00:06:00,592
Let me grab your
guitar from you there.

84
00:06:00,661 --> 00:06:02,026
Look at this!

85
00:06:02,095 --> 00:06:03,861
Whoo!

86
00:06:03,930 --> 00:06:05,697
Yeah. Hey.

87
00:06:07,767 --> 00:06:09,700
Wow!

88
00:06:09,769 --> 00:06:11,235
You been living here long?

89
00:06:11,304 --> 00:06:13,383
Oh, yeah, I was... I
was born in this town.

90
00:06:13,407 --> 00:06:15,151
Yeah, about five years
ago I decided to stay.

91
00:06:15,175 --> 00:06:16,686
So I went out and I...
I bought the biggest

92
00:06:16,710 --> 00:06:18,854
and best house I could find.

93
00:06:18,878 --> 00:06:20,356
I like what you've done with it.

94
00:06:20,380 --> 00:06:21,813
Thank you.

95
00:06:21,881 --> 00:06:23,859
Yeah, it's-it's interesting.

96
00:06:23,883 --> 00:06:25,850
Yeah. You hungry?

97
00:06:25,919 --> 00:06:27,730
I could eat
something. All right.

98
00:06:27,754 --> 00:06:30,314
All right, let's get
some food here.

99
00:06:30,757 --> 00:06:31,801
Here. Ooh!

100
00:06:31,825 --> 00:06:32,825
Look at this.

101
00:06:32,859 --> 00:06:34,125
All right!

102
00:06:34,194 --> 00:06:36,005
Look, um, help yourself.

103
00:06:36,029 --> 00:06:38,007
If you feel like cooking
anything, go right ahead.

104
00:06:38,031 --> 00:06:40,009
I gotta go talk
to the band here.

105
00:06:40,033 --> 00:06:41,967
Shouldn't take too long.

106
00:06:45,305 --> 00:06:47,271
Ooh.

107
00:07:12,366 --> 00:07:14,265
Thanks, Terry.

108
00:07:15,635 --> 00:07:17,702
Hey just... What? What?

109
00:07:17,771 --> 00:07:19,148
End of conversation, guys.

110
00:07:19,172 --> 00:07:21,172
I don't want to talk
about it. Terry...

111
00:07:21,241 --> 00:07:22,818
Look, just... would
you just leave, all right?

112
00:07:22,842 --> 00:07:24,442
All right!

113
00:07:24,511 --> 00:07:25,754
Who's that?

114
00:07:25,778 --> 00:07:27,757
A friend of mine.
Come on. Let's go.

115
00:07:27,781 --> 00:07:29,714
See you, Terry.

116
00:07:31,018 --> 00:07:32,929
Could you use a drink? I could.

117
00:07:32,953 --> 00:07:34,886
Anytime, man. All right.

118
00:07:34,955 --> 00:07:36,888
Sorry about that.

119
00:07:42,996 --> 00:07:45,497
Well... what's your
pleasure, my friend?

120
00:07:45,565 --> 00:07:48,500
Oh, a little rye
would be all right.

121
00:07:48,568 --> 00:07:50,502
A little rye would be all right.

122
00:07:50,570 --> 00:07:52,504
We can do a little rye here.

123
00:07:52,572 --> 00:07:53,782
Straight up for me.

124
00:07:53,806 --> 00:07:55,118
Straight up for you? Yeah.

125
00:07:55,142 --> 00:07:57,075
All right, I'll match you.

126
00:07:57,144 --> 00:07:59,077
There we are.

127
00:08:00,013 --> 00:08:01,946
All right.

128
00:08:02,015 --> 00:08:04,015
Well... Here's to it.

129
00:08:04,084 --> 00:08:05,950
All right.

130
00:08:06,019 --> 00:08:07,952
Mmm.

131
00:08:08,021 --> 00:08:10,455
You know, I'm gonna
write you a song.

132
00:08:10,524 --> 00:08:12,502
Ah. I'm gonna put
your name on it.

133
00:08:12,526 --> 00:08:14,158
That way, if it's a hit,

134
00:08:14,227 --> 00:08:15,927
you can live off the royalties.

135
00:08:15,996 --> 00:08:18,797
For a while, anyway.
Come on now.

136
00:08:18,866 --> 00:08:20,565
It can't be that easy.

137
00:08:20,633 --> 00:08:23,034
Well, people like me got
rich ripping off people like you.

138
00:08:23,102 --> 00:08:24,602
It's payback time,

139
00:08:24,671 --> 00:08:26,638
my friend. All right with me.

140
00:08:42,656 --> 00:08:44,589
♪ ♪

141
00:09:00,106 --> 00:09:01,973
Hello.

142
00:09:02,041 --> 00:09:03,475
Hi.

143
00:09:03,543 --> 00:09:05,410
I'm gonna be working
here a couple of hours.

144
00:09:05,478 --> 00:09:07,412
No problem.

145
00:09:12,452 --> 00:09:14,419
Nice!

146
00:09:17,624 --> 00:09:19,223
Yeah!

147
00:09:19,293 --> 00:09:21,270
Yeah, yeah. You know,
you're welcome to stay, Eddie.

148
00:09:21,294 --> 00:09:22,493
No.

149
00:09:22,562 --> 00:09:23,573
Your mother's coming back,

150
00:09:23,597 --> 00:09:25,196
and I don't want to
cause no problems.

151
00:09:25,265 --> 00:09:26,409
No, forget about that.

152
00:09:26,433 --> 00:09:28,366
Look, I'll be back in
the morning, okay?

153
00:09:28,435 --> 00:09:29,745
All right, where
you gonna sleep?

154
00:09:29,769 --> 00:09:32,537
Well, there's a big
oak tree in the park

155
00:09:32,606 --> 00:09:34,706
I sleep under and
it's real comfortable.

156
00:09:34,774 --> 00:09:36,040
All right.

157
00:09:36,109 --> 00:09:37,753
Can I take you
there, at least? No.

158
00:09:37,777 --> 00:09:39,711
I'll be back for
breakfast in the morning.

159
00:09:39,779 --> 00:09:40,789
How's that? All right.

160
00:09:40,813 --> 00:09:42,124
Breakfast is around noon.

161
00:09:42,148 --> 00:09:43,248
Okay.

162
00:09:43,316 --> 00:09:44,436
Thanks for everything, yeah?

163
00:09:44,484 --> 00:09:46,061
Good night, Eddie. Bye-bye.

164
00:09:46,085 --> 00:09:48,018
Bye.

165
00:09:49,922 --> 00:09:51,489
Good night, Eddie!

166
00:09:51,558 --> 00:09:52,723
Night!

167
00:09:56,496 --> 00:09:57,829
You coming in?

168
00:09:57,897 --> 00:10:00,231
Uh, you go ahead.

169
00:10:00,300 --> 00:10:01,900
I'll be in in a minute.

170
00:10:01,969 --> 00:10:03,901
All right.

171
00:11:33,126 --> 00:11:35,059
♪ ♪

172
00:11:35,128 --> 00:11:37,688
There he is!

173
00:11:41,467 --> 00:11:43,234
Hands against the tree!

174
00:11:43,302 --> 00:11:45,236
Come on. Up! Up!

175
00:11:45,305 --> 00:11:47,338
Hands against the tree! What?!

176
00:11:47,407 --> 00:11:49,740
What's going on?!
Spread your feet.

177
00:11:53,513 --> 00:11:56,380
Well, well.

178
00:11:56,449 --> 00:11:59,684
Look what we have here.

179
00:12:15,768 --> 00:12:16,801
Yeah.

180
00:12:16,870 --> 00:12:17,802
Ben.

181
00:12:17,871 --> 00:12:19,003
Dan Guerney.

182
00:12:19,072 --> 00:12:20,538
Sorry to call you so early.

183
00:12:20,606 --> 00:12:22,852
I wanted to catch you
before you left for the office.

184
00:12:22,876 --> 00:12:26,410
You did a good job, Dan.

185
00:12:26,479 --> 00:12:30,548
Ben... Ben?

186
00:12:30,616 --> 00:12:31,549
What?

187
00:12:31,617 --> 00:12:33,050
Got a murder out here.

188
00:12:33,120 --> 00:12:34,952
Thought it might interest you.

189
00:12:36,322 --> 00:12:40,425
Dan, I... I got all the
work I can handle in town.

190
00:12:40,493 --> 00:12:43,895
I think the DA's
got the wrong man.

191
00:12:43,963 --> 00:12:45,530
Ever hear of Eddie Haynes?

192
00:12:49,436 --> 00:12:52,670
Are you talking about
Eddie "Cougar" Haynes?

193
00:13:13,893 --> 00:13:16,827
♪ ♪

194
00:13:35,048 --> 00:13:38,149
Hello, Eddie, I'm Ben Matlock.

195
00:13:38,217 --> 00:13:41,752
Oh, the sheriff's friend.

196
00:13:41,821 --> 00:13:43,754
I like your music.

197
00:13:43,823 --> 00:13:46,723
I heard you once
in a club in Atlanta.

198
00:13:46,792 --> 00:13:48,392
That must have
been The Lion's Head.

199
00:13:48,461 --> 00:13:50,894
I always played The
Lion's Head in Atlanta.

200
00:13:50,964 --> 00:13:53,042
Yeah, that was a long time ago.

201
00:13:53,066 --> 00:13:54,131
Yeah.

202
00:13:54,200 --> 00:13:55,732
20 years, maybe more.

203
00:13:55,801 --> 00:13:59,370
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

204
00:13:59,439 --> 00:14:04,108
Eddie, uh, how'd you
wind up on the streets?

205
00:14:05,979 --> 00:14:08,245
Not taking care of business.

206
00:14:08,314 --> 00:14:12,149
Drinking too much,
partying too much.

207
00:14:12,218 --> 00:14:14,151
Anything else?

208
00:14:16,255 --> 00:14:19,991
Well, I did time...
Three to five for assault

209
00:14:20,059 --> 00:14:23,194
on some club owner
that stiffed me for my pay.

210
00:14:23,262 --> 00:14:26,530
When I come out, most
of the clubs were gone,

211
00:14:26,599 --> 00:14:29,867
and the ones that was
left... They wouldn't touch me.

212
00:14:29,935 --> 00:14:32,636
Oh, yeah.

213
00:14:32,705 --> 00:14:36,340
Uh, now, Eddie, uh,

214
00:14:36,409 --> 00:14:39,443
this, uh, police report says

215
00:14:39,512 --> 00:14:42,679
that you were found with
Terry Maslin's gold Rolex watch

216
00:14:42,748 --> 00:14:44,248
and several thousand dollars'

217
00:14:44,317 --> 00:14:46,850
worth of his jewelry.

218
00:14:48,954 --> 00:14:50,888
I don't steal.

219
00:14:50,956 --> 00:14:54,058
The, uh, the current theory is

220
00:14:54,127 --> 00:14:57,761
that, uh... Terry Maslin
caught you stealing his stuff,

221
00:14:57,830 --> 00:14:58,962
and you killed him.

222
00:14:59,032 --> 00:15:01,298
I don't steal,

223
00:15:01,367 --> 00:15:04,735
and when I left he was alive.

224
00:15:04,803 --> 00:15:06,470
Why'd you leave?

225
00:15:06,539 --> 00:15:09,673
I had a good thing
going with the boy.

226
00:15:09,742 --> 00:15:11,808
He was going to put
my name on a song,

227
00:15:11,877 --> 00:15:14,711
and I could live
off the royalties,

228
00:15:14,780 --> 00:15:15,891
but his mama didn't like me.

229
00:15:15,915 --> 00:15:17,881
She didn't want me around.

230
00:15:17,950 --> 00:15:19,561
I didn't want to
get her riled up,

231
00:15:19,585 --> 00:15:22,386
so I figured I'd spend
the night in the park,

232
00:15:22,455 --> 00:15:23,695
and then maybe I wouldn't have

233
00:15:23,722 --> 00:15:25,789
to spend any more
nights in the park.

234
00:15:25,858 --> 00:15:27,324
You know where I'm coming from?

235
00:15:27,393 --> 00:15:29,560
Yeah, yeah.

236
00:15:29,628 --> 00:15:32,729
Uh, who else knew
you were in the house?

237
00:15:32,798 --> 00:15:37,301
Well, like I said,
his mama, his band,

238
00:15:37,370 --> 00:15:39,114
and that girl I told
the police about.

239
00:15:39,138 --> 00:15:40,637
She was there when I left.

240
00:15:40,706 --> 00:15:45,242
The county police say
they have nothing to go on.

241
00:15:45,311 --> 00:15:48,345
They, they, they don't know
who this girl is, her name.

242
00:15:48,414 --> 00:15:51,382
They don't even
know if she exists.

243
00:15:51,451 --> 00:15:54,418
Well, she does.

244
00:15:54,487 --> 00:15:57,521
I might be a convicted felon,

245
00:15:57,590 --> 00:15:59,656
but I didn't kill nobody.

246
00:16:01,760 --> 00:16:03,994
Eddie, I believe you.

247
00:16:27,287 --> 00:16:30,721
<i>People of the state of
Georgia v. Edward Haynes.</i>

248
00:16:30,790 --> 00:16:33,357
<i>Case number 245104.</i>

249
00:16:33,426 --> 00:16:35,404
Benjamin Matlock for
the defense, Your Honor.

250
00:16:35,428 --> 00:16:36,727
Defendant is present

251
00:16:36,796 --> 00:16:40,197
and waives further reading
of his rights in the indictment.

252
00:16:40,266 --> 00:16:43,634
Pleads not guilty, requests
a jury trial, waives time.

253
00:16:43,703 --> 00:16:46,470
How about, uh, April 12?

254
00:16:46,539 --> 00:16:47,671
Fine.

255
00:16:47,740 --> 00:16:49,374
Prosecution agrees.

256
00:16:49,442 --> 00:16:51,743
As to the subject
of bail, Your Honor,

257
00:16:51,811 --> 00:16:53,731
defendant has no
means of support,

258
00:16:53,780 --> 00:16:54,812
no money.

259
00:16:54,881 --> 00:16:56,258
When arrested, he was
found sleeping in a park

260
00:16:56,282 --> 00:16:58,215
because, as he put it,

261
00:16:58,284 --> 00:17:00,451
he could not afford a room.

262
00:17:00,520 --> 00:17:02,431
Under the circumstances,
there's a real possibility

263
00:17:02,455 --> 00:17:04,522
Mr. Haynes might
flee jurisdiction,

264
00:17:04,591 --> 00:17:05,968
and I feel compelled
to recommend

265
00:17:05,992 --> 00:17:07,792
the highest possible bail.

266
00:17:07,861 --> 00:17:11,262
Uh, Your Honor, Eddie
"Cougar" Haynes' music

267
00:17:11,330 --> 00:17:13,464
is in the Smithsonian Institute.

268
00:17:13,533 --> 00:17:16,033
His music is in the
Library of Congress.

269
00:17:16,102 --> 00:17:20,371
No compilation of the blues
would be complete without him.

270
00:17:20,440 --> 00:17:21,472
He is a legend.

271
00:17:21,541 --> 00:17:23,340
He is a national treasure.

272
00:17:23,410 --> 00:17:25,843
Mr. Matlock, this
is all very true,

273
00:17:25,912 --> 00:17:27,378
but it misses the point.

274
00:17:27,446 --> 00:17:32,316
Your Honor, Eddie Haynes
is part of our cultural heritage.

275
00:17:32,385 --> 00:17:34,151
Surely he deserves to be treated

276
00:17:34,220 --> 00:17:36,053
with a modicum of respect.

277
00:17:36,122 --> 00:17:39,757
Mr. Matlock, the man
has no money, no home.

278
00:17:39,825 --> 00:17:42,393
If released, where
is he going to stay?

279
00:17:42,461 --> 00:17:44,028
With me.

280
00:17:44,097 --> 00:17:45,162
Fine.

281
00:17:45,231 --> 00:17:47,531
Stay at my house.
Yeah, I said all right.

282
00:17:47,600 --> 00:17:49,867
Defendant is released
on his own recognizance.

283
00:17:49,936 --> 00:17:51,535
Court is recessed.

284
00:18:06,619 --> 00:18:09,820
Well... there's no
furniture or chairs

285
00:18:09,889 --> 00:18:11,889
or anything. Mm-mm,
there is none.

286
00:18:11,957 --> 00:18:14,102
Terry Maslin liked it like this.

287
00:18:14,126 --> 00:18:15,126
Odd.

288
00:18:18,164 --> 00:18:20,698
Uh, Ben, this is Terry's
mother Lorraine Maslin.

289
00:18:20,766 --> 00:18:21,699
Hello.

290
00:18:21,767 --> 00:18:24,235
This is Ben Matlock.

291
00:18:24,304 --> 00:18:25,424
I think I'll be outside.

292
00:18:25,472 --> 00:18:29,373
Yes, um... I'm sorry
about your son.

293
00:18:29,441 --> 00:18:31,019
Then why are you
defending his killer?

294
00:18:31,043 --> 00:18:35,279
I don't think Eddie
Haynes killed anybody.

295
00:18:35,347 --> 00:18:38,783
But he had my son's
things... His watch, his jewelry.

296
00:18:38,851 --> 00:18:42,486
The, the diamond ring that
he had inherited from his father.

297
00:18:42,555 --> 00:18:45,289
He loved that ring.

298
00:18:45,358 --> 00:18:49,093
Why did he bring that
man back here with him?

299
00:18:49,162 --> 00:18:52,263
If he hadn't done that,
he'd be alive today.

300
00:19:04,611 --> 00:19:06,544
Um...

301
00:19:06,613 --> 00:19:11,115
w-was, was there a
woman here that night?

302
00:19:11,184 --> 00:19:14,051
I have no idea who was here.

303
00:19:15,155 --> 00:19:19,123
Was Terry seeing
someone special?

304
00:19:19,192 --> 00:19:21,993
There was a different
girl every week.

305
00:19:22,061 --> 00:19:24,161
You had to have a
program to keep track.

306
00:19:24,230 --> 00:19:27,231
Hmm.

307
00:19:27,300 --> 00:19:31,602
Uh, I understand, uh, he had
a meeting with his old band.

308
00:19:31,671 --> 00:19:33,838
They were all his friends.

309
00:19:33,906 --> 00:19:34,839
They grew up together.

310
00:19:34,908 --> 00:19:37,174
There was an argument?

311
00:19:37,243 --> 00:19:38,909
They were like a family.

312
00:19:38,978 --> 00:19:41,345
Sometimes families fight.

313
00:19:41,413 --> 00:19:43,346
It doesn't mean anything.

314
00:19:44,450 --> 00:19:47,718
Yes, ma'am.

315
00:19:47,787 --> 00:19:50,020
Uh, thank you, Ms. Maslin,

316
00:19:50,089 --> 00:19:53,758
and again, I'm very sorry.

317
00:20:23,189 --> 00:20:26,123
♪ ♪

318
00:20:33,699 --> 00:20:35,143
Nice.

319
00:20:35,167 --> 00:20:39,236
Hey, man, I don't like nobody
coming up on me like that.

320
00:20:39,305 --> 00:20:41,538
I'm sorry, I didn't
mean to surprise you.

321
00:20:41,607 --> 00:20:42,607
That's all right.

322
00:20:42,674 --> 00:20:44,353
Come on, sit down
and have a drink.

323
00:20:44,377 --> 00:20:46,410
All right, uh, sure, sure.

324
00:20:46,478 --> 00:20:49,980
You know, you said some
weird things about me in court.

325
00:20:50,049 --> 00:20:52,283
Well, I just, I just meant

326
00:20:52,351 --> 00:20:55,386
that you're a... fine artist.

327
00:20:55,454 --> 00:20:58,088
Well, I sure appreciate that.

328
00:20:58,157 --> 00:21:02,026
But I'm just a poor guit
picker from out of Mississippi.

329
00:21:02,094 --> 00:21:04,428
My people all
worked in the fields.

330
00:21:04,496 --> 00:21:07,431
Well, where'd you start singing?

331
00:21:07,499 --> 00:21:09,266
In church.

332
00:21:09,335 --> 00:21:12,236
I used to love
to sing in church.

333
00:21:12,305 --> 00:21:15,306
See the tears coming
down people's face,

334
00:21:15,374 --> 00:21:16,307
clapping their hands

335
00:21:16,375 --> 00:21:18,309
and patting their feet.

336
00:21:18,377 --> 00:21:20,522
You know you're
bringing joy to their hearts.

337
00:21:20,546 --> 00:21:24,081
Oh, there's, there's
nobody that sings

338
00:21:24,150 --> 00:21:26,190
and plays guitar the
way you do, Eddie.

339
00:21:26,252 --> 00:21:27,985
Oh, thank you.

340
00:21:28,054 --> 00:21:32,622
Um, I wonder if, uh...

341
00:21:32,691 --> 00:21:35,125
if it'd be all right...

342
00:21:35,194 --> 00:21:38,929
if I played with you a
little bit of something.

343
00:21:38,998 --> 00:21:40,842
Well, I see you got a guitar.

344
00:22:37,089 --> 00:22:39,023
That's a nice song.

345
00:22:39,091 --> 00:22:41,825
Wait till it's finished.

346
00:22:41,894 --> 00:22:43,305
If you're looking
for the gospel group,

347
00:22:43,329 --> 00:22:44,595
they're in studio C.

348
00:22:44,664 --> 00:22:47,131
Oh, thanks for
thinking I'd fit in.

349
00:22:47,199 --> 00:22:48,132
Ben Matlock.

350
00:22:48,200 --> 00:22:49,566
Oh, let me guess.

351
00:22:49,635 --> 00:22:51,301
You'd like to ask
me a few questions.

352
00:22:51,370 --> 00:22:53,015
Yes. Like "Why was the band

353
00:22:53,039 --> 00:22:55,372
over at Terry's house
the day he was killed?"

354
00:22:55,441 --> 00:22:56,751
Yeah. We were
there to discuss a tour.

355
00:22:56,775 --> 00:22:57,775
Uh-huh.

356
00:22:57,843 --> 00:23:00,010
Uh, was, uh... was
there an argument?

357
00:23:00,079 --> 00:23:01,879
Down and out, man.

358
00:23:01,948 --> 00:23:03,046
What about?

359
00:23:03,116 --> 00:23:05,649
Keith wanted to
go out on the road,

360
00:23:05,718 --> 00:23:07,184
and Terry didn't.

361
00:23:07,253 --> 00:23:11,088
There's a lot of money in
those big rock and roll tours.

362
00:23:11,157 --> 00:23:12,089
Millions.

363
00:23:12,158 --> 00:23:15,659
And Terry didn't care?

364
00:23:15,728 --> 00:23:18,428
Terry was as rich as Croesus.

365
00:23:18,497 --> 00:23:21,565
He owned the publishing on our
music, so he didn't need a tour.

366
00:23:21,634 --> 00:23:23,333
What about you?

367
00:23:23,402 --> 00:23:25,636
What about me?
Did you need a tour,

368
00:23:25,704 --> 00:23:27,337
Mr. Chiarro?

369
00:23:27,406 --> 00:23:30,408
What I need is to turn
this album into a big hit.

370
00:23:30,476 --> 00:23:31,942
If the tour had coincided

371
00:23:32,011 --> 00:23:33,956
with the album's release,
it would have been perfect.

372
00:23:33,980 --> 00:23:36,647
That's what I told Terry.

373
00:23:37,749 --> 00:23:40,217
Well, you've been a big help.

374
00:23:40,286 --> 00:23:43,954
Uh, uh, was there
a girl at the house

375
00:23:44,023 --> 00:23:46,201
that day? I didn't see her.

376
00:23:46,225 --> 00:23:48,492
Was Terry dating
anybody special?

377
00:23:50,029 --> 00:23:52,563
Terry changed girls the
way he changed his shirts.

378
00:23:52,631 --> 00:23:54,598
You know? Like, every day.

379
00:23:54,667 --> 00:23:56,310
There's no way I'd
know who it was.

380
00:23:56,334 --> 00:24:00,804
Oh, well... Thanks.

381
00:24:02,274 --> 00:24:04,842
♪ ♪

382
00:24:10,583 --> 00:24:13,784
Yes, I was angry.
I wanted a tour.

383
00:24:13,853 --> 00:24:16,286
For the money?

384
00:24:16,355 --> 00:24:19,757
Honey, do I look
like I need money?

385
00:24:19,826 --> 00:24:22,359
Well... why?

386
00:24:22,428 --> 00:24:26,029
I'm a drummer, and a
drummer needs a band.

387
00:24:26,098 --> 00:24:27,976
I never thought about that.

388
00:24:28,000 --> 00:24:29,767
We're usually not songwriters,

389
00:24:29,836 --> 00:24:30,934
usually not singers.

390
00:24:31,003 --> 00:24:33,370
When a band breaks
up, we go home.

391
00:24:33,439 --> 00:24:35,606
I've been home for three years.

392
00:24:35,675 --> 00:24:37,107
That's why I wanted a tour.

393
00:24:37,176 --> 00:24:38,943
I want some fun.

394
00:24:39,011 --> 00:24:40,944
I wanted something to do.

395
00:24:41,013 --> 00:24:43,573
Why did your band,
Dreams, break up?

396
00:24:43,616 --> 00:24:45,515
The party line on the break,

397
00:24:45,584 --> 00:24:47,351
is "artistic differences."

398
00:24:47,420 --> 00:24:50,120
And the truth?

399
00:24:50,189 --> 00:24:52,622
The truth is, three years ago,

400
00:24:52,691 --> 00:24:54,791
Paul Chiarro's wife left him.

401
00:24:54,860 --> 00:24:56,693
Yeah?

402
00:24:56,762 --> 00:25:01,065
She left Paul for his
oldest and dearest friend...

403
00:25:01,133 --> 00:25:03,400
And business
partner, Terry Maslin.

404
00:25:04,971 --> 00:25:08,138
Brianne Chiarro was an
airhead of the first order.

405
00:25:08,207 --> 00:25:11,575
She actually thought she
could make Terry settle down.

406
00:25:12,744 --> 00:25:14,411
It lasted three weeks,

407
00:25:14,480 --> 00:25:16,413
and destroyed the band forever.

408
00:25:17,816 --> 00:25:19,183
You met Paul?

409
00:25:19,251 --> 00:25:20,184
Not yet.

410
00:25:20,252 --> 00:25:22,419
Thanks.

411
00:25:22,487 --> 00:25:24,922
I love Paul.

412
00:25:24,990 --> 00:25:27,157
But Paul is
slightly obsessive...

413
00:25:27,225 --> 00:25:31,061
uh, possessive and all
those good macho things.

414
00:25:31,129 --> 00:25:32,729
He went berserk.

415
00:25:32,798 --> 00:25:34,731
The band went to pieces.

416
00:25:34,800 --> 00:25:37,000
And that, as they say, was that.

417
00:25:37,069 --> 00:25:39,636
You think Paul killed Terry?

418
00:25:39,705 --> 00:25:43,040
Sounds to me like<i> you</i>
think Paul killed Terry.

419
00:25:49,715 --> 00:25:52,082
♪ ♪

420
00:26:09,468 --> 00:26:10,867
What is he doing here?

421
00:26:10,936 --> 00:26:12,447
You didn't say you were
going to bring him here!

422
00:26:12,471 --> 00:26:15,372
We can't find the girl who
was here the other night.

423
00:26:15,440 --> 00:26:18,341
And I thought if I could
bring Eddie back out here,

424
00:26:18,410 --> 00:26:19,977
it might somehow jog

425
00:26:20,046 --> 00:26:21,612
his memory. I want you to leave.

426
00:26:21,681 --> 00:26:24,247
Ms. Maslin, the girl

427
00:26:24,316 --> 00:26:26,394
might be our only
hope for clearing Eddie.

428
00:26:26,418 --> 00:26:28,699
Please, this would only
take a few minutes, please?

429
00:26:30,322 --> 00:26:32,422
Five minutes. Thank
you. Yes, ma'am.

430
00:26:35,761 --> 00:26:37,038
Where was the girl?

431
00:26:37,062 --> 00:26:39,829
Right over there, about
this high, dark hair,

432
00:26:39,899 --> 00:26:41,097
good body.

433
00:26:41,166 --> 00:26:42,599
What was she wearing?

434
00:26:44,003 --> 00:26:47,771
Dungarees, tight, black
turtleneck sweater...

435
00:26:49,108 --> 00:26:50,819
Come to think of
it, she didn't wear

436
00:26:50,843 --> 00:26:51,953
that turtleneck when I left.

437
00:26:51,977 --> 00:26:53,755
She had on a shirt, big shirt,

438
00:26:53,779 --> 00:26:55,445
like it wasn't hers.

439
00:26:58,350 --> 00:26:59,950
We got five minutes.

440
00:27:07,459 --> 00:27:10,561
Boy's got enough clothes in
here to open up his own mall.

441
00:27:12,230 --> 00:27:14,164
Not in here.

442
00:27:19,171 --> 00:27:21,138
How about that?

443
00:27:21,206 --> 00:27:23,740
Pay dirt.

444
00:27:23,809 --> 00:27:25,742
Okay, let's see...

445
00:27:41,893 --> 00:27:43,160
What do you think you're doing?

446
00:27:43,228 --> 00:27:45,273
I never said you could
go upstairs. Yes, ma'am.

447
00:27:45,297 --> 00:27:47,564
I never even said you
could come into the house!

448
00:27:53,004 --> 00:27:54,938
And don't ever come back!

449
00:28:05,183 --> 00:28:07,383
Ah. Well, back again.

450
00:28:07,452 --> 00:28:09,820
Interesting place. Yeah.

451
00:28:09,888 --> 00:28:11,599
No, you know what the problem is?
- What?

452
00:28:11,623 --> 00:28:13,501
It's the same as always.
You're playing too loud.

453
00:28:13,525 --> 00:28:15,625
Rock and roll. Hey! Gentlemen!

454
00:28:15,695 --> 00:28:17,405
Hey, hey, hey! My new groupie.

455
00:28:17,429 --> 00:28:19,374
Mr. Matlock, have you met
the rest of the band? No.

456
00:28:19,398 --> 00:28:21,143
This is Keith Morrissey. Hello.

457
00:28:21,167 --> 00:28:22,644
Dennis Johnson,
Ben Matlock. Yes, sir.

458
00:28:22,668 --> 00:28:24,646
Hey. You're planning a tour?

459
00:28:24,670 --> 00:28:25,936
Word gets around.

460
00:28:26,005 --> 00:28:28,204
This should be the
biggest one ever.

461
00:28:28,273 --> 00:28:31,008
A farewell memorial
tour, big auditoriums...

462
00:28:31,076 --> 00:28:33,776
Major dollars,
Mr. Matlock. Yeah.

463
00:28:33,845 --> 00:28:36,746
You get your way, Mr. Morrissey.

464
00:28:36,815 --> 00:28:39,083
I guess so.

465
00:28:39,151 --> 00:28:40,850
Did you want something?

466
00:28:40,919 --> 00:28:45,355
Uh, well, I, I was hoping, uh...

467
00:28:45,424 --> 00:28:46,868
we might talk. PAUL: Uh...

468
00:28:46,892 --> 00:28:48,024
Well, we'll go inside.

469
00:28:48,093 --> 00:28:50,127
Okay.

470
00:28:50,195 --> 00:28:52,195
Good. Good, thanks.

471
00:28:52,264 --> 00:28:53,696
All right.

472
00:28:53,765 --> 00:28:57,005
What can I do for you,
Mr. Matlock? Well...

473
00:28:58,169 --> 00:29:00,448
Uh, the other
members of the band

474
00:29:00,472 --> 00:29:02,205
live in mansions.

475
00:29:02,274 --> 00:29:03,554
You rent an apartment.

476
00:29:03,609 --> 00:29:07,143
They drive big cars
and you're in a compact.

477
00:29:08,280 --> 00:29:09,679
I'm broke.

478
00:29:09,748 --> 00:29:11,681
Why is that?

479
00:29:11,750 --> 00:29:14,718
Bad investments, bad habits.

480
00:29:14,787 --> 00:29:18,021
Then you really
wanted that tour.

481
00:29:18,090 --> 00:29:20,490
Right again.

482
00:29:20,559 --> 00:29:23,126
Unless I miss my guess,

483
00:29:23,195 --> 00:29:25,795
you were<i> desperate</i>
for that tour,

484
00:29:25,864 --> 00:29:28,965
because you're a gambler.

485
00:29:31,170 --> 00:29:34,071
I don't advertise my
problems, Mr. Matlock.

486
00:29:34,139 --> 00:29:35,772
Terry was my friend.

487
00:29:35,840 --> 00:29:37,452
He was the one
that bailed me out,

488
00:29:37,476 --> 00:29:39,609
got me the help I
needed to stop gambling.

489
00:29:39,678 --> 00:29:41,211
Well, that's honest.

490
00:29:41,280 --> 00:29:42,890
I know how he felt about a tour.

491
00:29:42,914 --> 00:29:44,426
There was no point
in arguing with him.

492
00:29:44,450 --> 00:29:46,950
He'd made up his mind.

493
00:29:47,018 --> 00:29:49,419
And you just accepted that?

494
00:29:49,488 --> 00:29:51,421
Had no choice.

495
00:29:53,658 --> 00:29:55,592
Thank you.

496
00:29:57,429 --> 00:29:59,062
You've got to give Denny credit.

497
00:29:59,131 --> 00:30:01,431
He's good. Yeah.

498
00:30:01,500 --> 00:30:02,877
<i>I understand you were</i>

499
00:30:02,901 --> 00:30:05,068
<i>at Terry Maslin's the
night he was murdered.</i>

500
00:30:05,137 --> 00:30:07,470
And who, may I
ask, told you that?

501
00:30:07,540 --> 00:30:10,473
Eddie Haynes
described you perfectly.

502
00:30:10,543 --> 00:30:12,609
He says you can clear him.

503
00:30:13,712 --> 00:30:16,246
I'm sorry. I wasn't there.

504
00:30:16,315 --> 00:30:17,680
I've never been there.

505
00:30:17,749 --> 00:30:20,417
Well, do you know
him? Ever met him?

506
00:30:20,486 --> 00:30:21,518
No.

507
00:30:21,587 --> 00:30:23,753
Then how did your sweater

508
00:30:23,822 --> 00:30:25,622
get in his bedroom?

509
00:30:27,426 --> 00:30:30,359
There must be a
million sweaters like that.

510
00:30:31,463 --> 00:30:34,064
This one has your
laundry mark in it.

511
00:30:36,201 --> 00:30:39,102
See? That's how I found you.

512
00:30:39,171 --> 00:30:42,738
Look, I have no idea
how my sweater got there.

513
00:30:42,807 --> 00:30:45,008
I only know that I
didn't put it there.

514
00:30:45,077 --> 00:30:47,076
All right?

515
00:30:47,145 --> 00:30:49,979
So, I'm leaving now.

516
00:30:50,048 --> 00:30:52,148
Suzanne.

517
00:30:52,217 --> 00:30:54,918
Look, if there's some reason
you can't tell me the truth,

518
00:30:54,986 --> 00:30:56,486
maybe I can protect you.

519
00:30:57,956 --> 00:30:59,722
What is it with you?

520
00:30:59,792 --> 00:31:01,724
I am telling the truth.

521
00:31:03,995 --> 00:31:05,995
I'll get a subpoena.

522
00:31:06,064 --> 00:31:08,298
We'll see if they
believe you in court.

523
00:31:08,367 --> 00:31:10,300
Excuse me.

524
00:31:26,819 --> 00:31:28,752
♪ ♪

525
00:31:30,689 --> 00:31:32,955
♪ How long? ♪

526
00:31:33,024 --> 00:31:35,458
♪ Baby, how long ♪

527
00:31:35,527 --> 00:31:40,463
♪ Has that evenin'
train been gone? ♪

528
00:31:40,532 --> 00:31:43,300
♪ How long? ♪

529
00:31:43,368 --> 00:31:47,470
♪ How long, baby, how long? ♪

530
00:31:50,909 --> 00:31:53,143
♪ If I could holler ♪

531
00:31:53,212 --> 00:31:55,645
♪ Like a mountain jack ♪

532
00:31:55,714 --> 00:31:58,048
♪ Go up on the mountain ♪

533
00:31:58,117 --> 00:32:00,028
♪ Call my baby back ♪
♪ Call my baby back ♪

534
00:32:00,052 --> 00:32:03,086
